  "root_cause": {
    "finding": "G148 solves same-kernel residency but not heterogeneous full-graph compatibility.",
    "explanation": "The isolated S2 loop keeps both lanes on the same QKV kernel, so their 114.82KiB CTAs can occupy the two proven slots. In the real graph the lanes frequently reach QKV against FA4 (about 232KiB), no-AB12 FFN (about 181KiB), or other resource-heavy GEMMs. A 148-CTA persistent grid spreads one long-lived CTA over every SM; those heterogeneous peers cannot co-reside with it. The scheduler can overlap only partial-grid tails as SMs become free. Valuable QKV-to-FFN and QKV-to-other-2SM overlap therefore collapses even though the QKV boundary itself does 6.68% less summed work.",
    "why_not_more_g148_tuning": "Grid/stage changes would retest already-closed resource points or abandon the one-CTA/lane hypothesis. The failure is heterogeneous kernel lifetime, not G148 load imbalance, lookup layout, spills, or numerical correctness."
  },
